<!--
    RE-friction round 4, need `argument-recovery-options-still` (challenge
    69761b7a39e9c4d85c2f9fc1, ELF x86-64 `graphy`): `kuna decompile ...
    sub_1002c90` calls one helper fifteen times and renders eleven of them with
    five arguments and four with three, from instruction-for-instruction
    identical code:

        sub_1005250(&v24,v22 & 0xffffffff,v8 & 0xff);                      0x1003470
        sub_1005250(&v24,v22 & 0xffffffff,v8 & 0xff,v38,sub_1004ef0(..));  0x1003750

    Root cause (P4, trial finalization).  Argument recovery scores each trial
    from the CALLER's data flow alone, and `Funcdata::onlyOpUse`
    (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) rejects a trial whose Varnode has any competing
    use - a CBRANCH, a LOAD, a STORE - anywhere in the function.  When the
    rejected trial is the LAST recovered argument, `ParamListStandard::fillinMap`
    drops it and every argument behind it, so the site renders short.

    `calleearity` and `calleearityfwd` exist for exactly this and both decline,
    because both refuse a call that recovered ANY argument.  That limit is not
    arbitrary: the whole-corpus sweep that bought it measured `Sleep(200)`
    becoming `Sleep(200,0)` from an over-recovered sibling, and a variadic
    internal logger `sub_1b11c(5,0,"Zip: empty archive?")` gaining two arguments
    its format string has no conversions for.  A sibling alone cannot tell a
    shorter call from a broken one.

    What can is the CALLEE'S OWN BODY, decoded by the same bounded walk
    `calleedeadarg` takes for the subtractive direction, and read for two things:
    every register the witness claims beyond this site's list is read before
    written by the callee, AND no other argument register of the model is.  A
    fixed-arity callee reads exactly the registers its prototype names; an import
    has no body, and a variadic register-save prologue reads every argument
    register there is.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "trial-finalization".  The P0 assertion
    `option calleearitylive on|off` decides whether that extension happens; it
    reuses `calleearity`'s refusals, is inert with `calleearity off`, and is
    deferred to the end of `ActionActiveParam::apply` because on the witness the
    four short sites are the FIRST four.

    The fixture is the same construct on SysV x86-64, in two halves:

        callee:  mov %rdi,%rax; add %esi,%eax; add %edx,%eax; add %ecx,%eax; ret
        callee2: the same, plus add %r8d,%eax

        caller / caller2, identically shaped:
                mov  %rsi,%r8 ; mov %rdx,%r9
                lea  0x8000(%r9),%ecx      # the 4th argument
                mov  $4,%esi ; mov %r8,%rdx
                cmp  $0xffff,%ecx          # ...which is also branched on
                ja   L2
                call callee                # SHORT: the ecx trial is rejected
                ret
        L2:     shr  $0x10,%r9
                lea  0x8000(%r9),%ecx      # a fresh ecx, no competing use
                mov  $4,%esi ; mov %r8,%rdx
                call callee                # WITNESS: four arguments
                ret

    `caller` is the fix and `caller2` is the discriminator.  They differ only in
    which callee they call: `callee2` also reads `r8d`, a register the
    four-argument witness does not claim, so the callee's body contradicts the
    witness and the extension is declined - the same refusal a variadic callee
    gets.  Pass 1 (`option calleearitylive off`) reproduces the BUG in both.
    Pass 2 (`option calleearitylive on`) fixes `caller` and leaves `caller2`
    exactly as it was.
-->
